STATEMENT: >>>
presentation.sgra -- the human reasoning layer, way under-specced by the
operator's own account ("I cannot even fathom the nodes right now; scope
could be overblown, it's blurry"). Use cases they named:

- Prose over nodes: spec.sgra is LLM-understandable; presentation is the
  human connecting the dots -- their reasoning layer.
- Ordering/hierarchy, the way strictdoc's HTML view does it.
- Custom axes for reasoning -- much of what should model the whiteboard.
- Data views: pivot tables and queries that feed graphs; the Perspective
  view is how the operator surfaces candidates.
- Outputs: markdown (README, docs, even skills), the whiteboard for
  non-markdown, maybe code -- code bindings not visible yet before other
  things settle.
- Maybe dirty relationships: a neighbor changed, so this needs review.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Two clauses kept verbatim because I cannot pin them down:
- "custom axis for reasoning. a lot of what I hope helps model the
  whiteboard."
- "maybe dirty relationships? neighbor changed so needs review." -- possible
  overlap with the existing PARENT_FP fingerprint machinery, which already
  marks a citing node suspect when the cited contract changes; whether this
  is that mechanism generalized, or something else, is a grooming question.
